-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-- **Spatial Quality Imbalance:** Global ranking methods can lead to situations where the limited budget concentrates in regions with consistently higher scores, leaving other regions with insufficient representational capacity [S1, unofficial]. SplatQ mitigates this via blockwise quota smoothing [S1, unofficial].
-- **Joint Deletion Effects:** Independent scoring fails to capture joint rendering effects because multiple Gaussians may jointly support the same pixel or make overlapping contributions [S1, unofficial]. Degradation from removing multiple Gaussians is not strictly additive [S1, unofficial]. SplatQ addresses this by modeling pairwise interactions via a quadratic objective [S1, unofficial].
-- **Scalability:** Formulating the optimization problem over the entire model requires $N$ binary variables and an $N \times N$ matrix, which is impractical for millions of Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. SplatQ decomposes this into smaller subproblems per block [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Spatial Quality Imbalance:** Global ranking methods may concentrate the budget in regions with consistently higher scores, leaving other regions with insufficient representational capacity [S1, unofficial]. SplatQ mitigates this via quota smoothing [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Joint Deletion Effects:** Independent scoring fails to capture that multiple Gaussians may jointly support the same pixel, meaning degradation from removing multiple Gaussians is not strictly additive [S1, unofficial]. SplatQ addresses this by modeling pairwise interactions in the QUBO objective [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Computational Complexity:** The QUBO problem is NP-hard. For large blocks, solving it may be slow. The block size $M$ must be chosen to balance optimization accuracy and computational cost [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The partitioning step uses 3D grid and Morton ordering to avoid quadratic pairwise comparisons [S1, unofficial]. The QUBO solving step is performed per block, with complexity dependent on block size $M$ and the solver (SA or CIM) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is reduced by decomposing the global $N \times N$ interaction matrix into smaller block-wise response matrices $R_b$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Ensures balanced reconstruction quality across the scene by controlling regional budget allocation [S1, unofficial]. Captures joint deletion effects and pairwise interactions, leading to better quality under aggressive pruning compared to independent scoring methods [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Requires additional computational overhead for partitioning, response matrix computation, and QUBO solving [S1, unofficial]. The quality of the pruning depends on the accuracy of the response matrix estimation [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When balanced spatial quality is critical and the scene contains regions with varying importance or density, and when standard global Top-K pruning results in visible artifacts or quality loss in less prominent areas [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The partitioning step is approximately linear in $N$ due to the use of 3D grids and Morton ordering [S1, unofficial]. The response matrix computation depends on the number of sampled pixels and block size $M$ ($O(|P| \cdot M^2)$ per block, where $|P|$ is the number of pixels) [S1, unofficial]. The QUBO solving step is exponential in $M$ in the worst case, but practical solvers like SA or CIM scale with $M$ [S1, unofficial]. Overall, it is more expensive than global Top-K but faster than full re-optimization.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the response matrices $R_b$ for all blocks, which is $O(\sum_b M^2)$. Since $M \ll N$, this is significantly less than an $N \times N$ matrix [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Ensures balanced reconstruction quality across the scene; accounts for joint deletion effects; post-training (no additional training required for pruning decision).
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of block size $M$ and quota smoothing parameters; QUBO solving can be slow for very large $M$; requires multiple views for response matrix estimation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When balanced visual quality across the entire scene is critical and the scene has varying density or complexity.
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources for the pruning phase are extremely limited, or when a simple global pruning is sufficient for the application.
 
 ## Background
-SplatQ was proposed by Hongbin Ye, Haixu Liu, Shenwei Kang, Wenxiu Wei, Tinglan Wang, Hongdong Zhu, Fan Tang, Qi Gao, Yin Ma, Kai Wen, Hai Wei, Nuo Qian, Wei-Shi Zheng, Shaobo Chen, Fa-Ting Hong, and Jingke Meng [S1, unofficial]. It was presented as an arXiv preprint (arXiv:2610.13503v1) on October 9, 2026 [S1, unofficial]. The work addresses limitations in existing 3D Gaussian Splatting pruning methods, which primarily rely on global Top-K selection based on independent importance scores [S1, unofficial].
+SplatQ was proposed by Hongbin Ye, Haixu Liu, Shenwei Kang, et al. in 2026 [S1, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in existing 3DGS pruning methods that rely on global Top-K selection based on independent importance scores, which lack regional budget control and ignore joint deletion effects [S1, unofficial]. The method is presented as a conference paper under review at ICLR 2027 [S1, unofficial].
 
